  "body": "## Answer: yes — and this time the mechanism is identified, not inferred.\n\nv1.10.18 shipped a working `RootErrorBoundary` (verified present in the bundle) and the screen was **still** black. That result is itself the decisive evidence: **the crash is not a render error at all.**\n\n## Root cause\n\nReact error boundaries only catch errors thrown during **render**. An error thrown from a timer, an event handler, a native callback, or any async continuation never reaches them.\n\nReact Native routes those to `ErrorUtils`' global handler. When it is called with `isFatal=true`, the **default handler tears down the JS context**. The Android Activity stays alive — so the system status bar and navigation bar keep drawing — but the React root view is emptied.\n\nThe result is exactly the reported screen:\n\n- black content area\n- system bars still visible\n- no crash dialog\n- no error text\n- **no logs** — the JS that would report it is already dead\n\nThis is why every previous fix failed. v1.10.16 (web-only), v1.10.17 (removed a native hazard) and v1.10.18 (render boundary) were all real defects, but none could catch a **non-render fatal error**.\n\n## Fix — Fatal Error Shield\n\nInstalled at **module scope** in the root layout, before any provider, timer, or feature code can run:\n\n- Intercepts the `ErrorUtils` global handler.\n- **Never forwards `isFatal=true`.** Forwarding it is what lets the default handler destroy the runtime and blank the screen. It is downgraded to non-fatal so React stays alive.\n- Surfaces the error as React state and renders an **IVX Runtime Error** screen with the full message, stack trace, and a Retry button.\n- Force-hides the splash in case the error fires before first paint.\n\n`RootErrorBoundary` is retained — it still covers render errors. The two together now cover both classes of failure.\n\n## Build stamp\n\nThe loading screen and error screen now display `Build 1.10.19 (117)`, so the installed binary is identifiable on